ming across now are often  from eastern ukraine some of them have  suffered war wounds almost all of them  are suffering trauma that's different  than when i was there where they were  coming mainly from western ukraine so  that vulnerability uh proposes it poses  a protection risk to be sure and we just  need to make sure that we  the international community has staffed  up sufficiently so that uh people know  uh what to avoid and and that there are  you know law enforcement and others  around  trying to uh you know hone in on any any  predators or people who'd be taking  advantage of this horrible situation  madam ambassador you're right to point  out the tremendous humanitarian spirit  the effort to help people coming over  the border there in poland in particular  at the same time some of the refugees i  spoke with last week were worried that  at some point the welcome will be worn  out and i was wondering if you could  talk a bit about on that subject kind of  the medium to long-term challenge of  handling the need of now nearly three  million people uh coming into new  countries  uh it's an overwhelming challenge  many of the people again especially in  these later waves don't speak any  language other than ukraine  for the kids they're getting absorbed  into european schools it's very  beautiful actually to watch  for example italian kids yesterday  welcoming  ukrainian kids and and so that  adjustment now that europe has decided  to make school benefits work benefits  healthcare benefits  uh available to people coming across  that part should be taken care of by  europe  but the longer term challenge is again  these are women and children the men are  left behind their hearts are breaking  they're living in constant fear of the  phone ringing and getting the worst  possible news and you don't really start  your family life when your family is  separated in that way the other  challenge tony is just your numbers uh i  mean this goes well beyond even the  wors